#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1080.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180573} :activity/id #uuid "210826c6-78d3-519b-84ab-0d8eb2721083" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050766 :topic/id #uuid "64ed08fd-258d-5456-ab85-4c3d693a62d2" :topic/math-academy-id 3687} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180573 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180764 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we'll discuss the concepts of **work** and **kinetic energy** and describe how these concepts are related. \n\nThe **work done by the force** $F=F(x)$ in moving a particle from the position $x=x_1$ to the position $x=x_2$ along the $x$-axis is defined as\n\n$$\nW = \\int_{x_1}^{x_2} F(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhen the force and distance are measured in Newtons ($\\textrm{N}$) and meters ($\\textrm{m}$), respectively, the work done by the force is measured in Joules ($\\rm{J}$).\n\nFor example, suppose a particle $P$ is moving along the $x$-axis under the action of a force of magnitude $4x^3\\,\\textrm{N},$ acting in the direction of increasing $x,$ as shown below. Distances along the $x$-axis are measured in meters.\n\n![](images/3f/3f0040d7a71563304cb954908988c82df1774a1197a5ce16c74a3b8ac65e04a0.png)\n\n \n\nLet's calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=1$ to $x=2.$\n\nSubstituting the given values into our formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{4}\\,{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =16-1 \\\\  & =15\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the work done by $F$ in moving $P$ from $x_1=1\\,\\textrm m$ to $x_2=2\\,\\textrm m$ equals $15\\,\\textrm J.$\n\nWe can use the concept of work in conjunction with Newton's second law. Let's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "33cf6fa3-5f8d-579b-990b-ddeb9d1e3b8a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7799 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "fa2ce148-bfaa-55f3-a775-56631c5bd980" :step/math-academy-id 45122 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180574}} 