:knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179440 :question/id #uuid "39eab788-1a8d-51af-b535-594cacc8ec2e" :question/math-academy-id "e-12527"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "94904fa2-bf02-5486-950c-979a4d12998e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180103} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180105} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284400} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284401} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284402} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376131} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389428} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389435} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389442} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416260} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416263} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416268}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Work-Energy Principle To Find the Work Done by a Force"} :step/id #uuid "496c1449-0914-5643-b8b4-95ec09fb5867" :step/math-academy-id 45125 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180577}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180577 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179780 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179441 :question/id #uuid "8f79d7e9-377f-5012-a762-279839fc806a" :question/math-academy-id "e-12528"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "793154ca-29f0-5552-97cd-57acbc915966" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048187}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180106} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180107} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284404} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284406} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284408} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284409} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284410} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284411} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389456} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416265} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416266} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416269}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Work-Energy Principle to Find a Particle's Velocity"} :step/id #uuid "b02a7662-acf4-57da-98d7-bae7093076f2" :step/math-academy-id 45126} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180578 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180765 :tutorial/content "The **kinetic energy** of a particle with mass $m$ and velocity $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{KE} = \\dfrac12mv^2.\n$$\n\nWhen the mass and velocity are measured in kilograms ($\\rm{kg}$) and meters per second ($\\rm{m/s}$), respectively, the particle's kinetic energy is measured in Joules ($\\rm{J}$). \n\nFor example, suppose a particle $P$ of mass $5\\,\\rm{kg}$ is moving along the $x$-axis from rest at the origin with an acceleration of $3t^2\\,\\rm{m/s}^2,$ where $t \\geq 0$ is the time in seconds. Let's calculate the particle's kinetic energy at time $t=2\\,\\rm{s}.$\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =\\int 3{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & ={t}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n0 = 0^3 + C\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nC = 0.\n$$\n\nHence, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t) = t^3.$ In particular, its velocity at time $t=2\\,\\rm{s}$ is\n\n$$\nv=v(2) = 2^3 = 8\\,\\rm{m/s}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the kinetic energy of the particle at $t=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{KE} & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot {8}^{2} \\\\  & =160\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- Kinetic energy is a *scalar* quantity.\n\n- You probably noticed that kinetic energy and work have the same units. This is not a coincidence! We'll learn more about that shortly.\n\n- In practical applications, it's often more important to calculate a *change* in the kinetic energy of a particle between two points in time. \n\nThe **change in kinetic energy** of a particle with mass $m,$ **initial velocity** $v_0,$ and **final velocity** $v_1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta \\, \\text{KE} = \\dfrac12mv_1^2 - \\dfrac12mv_0^2.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "42f83ae3-3805-546b-a5bf-264b8b56d6ef" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7811 :tutorial/title "Kinetic Energy"} :step/id #uuid "3bec1351-7ee4-5f29-8760-5dd679edda2e" :step/math-academy-id 45179 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180575}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180579 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180766 :tutorial/content "The **work-energy principle** states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW = \\Delta\\, \\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta\\, \\text{KE} = \\dfrac12 m v_1^2 - \\dfrac12 m v_0^2\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, $v_0$ is its initial velocity, and $v_1$ is its final velocity." :tutorial/id #uuid "e00f462b-c087-5c42-afc7-bfeffad5906b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7812 :tutorial/title "The Work-Energy Principle"} :step/id #uuid "4d3d3f00-8ddb-5882-984a-2f48d2b19da6" :step/math-academy-id 45180 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180576}}] :activity/title "The Work-Energy Principle" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179842}]}
